Z||000000||2farnorth||04-28-2001||01:45 PM||||I have an 85 2.5, 5 speed that has slugish acceleration until it reaches 2800 to 3000 rpm.  Then it takes off like a scalded cat.<BR>It does it consistently in all gears.<BR>I have been swapping parts from another engine trying to eliminate the problem, but no luck.  I've been thru the search option several times and in theogres cave trying to find help.<BR>I've changed : tps, egr, map, oxy sensor, iac, fuel injector and pres reg. swapped distributers, checked and rechecked timing, searched for vacume leaks.<BR>None of these items had any effect on the problem.  I know it's not normal because my<BR>wife has one also and it has "good" acceleration.  Thanks.<P><BR>Dave.||||reg||5
Z||000001||Mach10||04-28-2001||02:09 PM||||How about Catalytic converter, Fuel filter, and Air filter? Your cyl. head might be gunked up w. carbon...<BR>Dunno...||||reg||5
Z||000002||2farnorth||04-28-2001||03:35 PM||||Air and fuel filters both new.  As far as the cat, it seems to me that if it was restricted I'd have more trouble at the upper rpms rather than the lower.  It pulls good between 3000 and 4500.  I thought about the fuel pump, but there again a weak fuel pump should cause worse performance up in the upper R's.  Maybe I'm wrong.  I'm about ready to try anything.<P>Dave||||reg||1
Z||000003||Mach10||04-28-2001||04:36 PM||||I test-drove a cadaver (Cavalier) with the 3.1, that had similar symptoms, except that it would hesitate as well... Hesitate, then start to move, and right around 3500rpm, it would be like someone hit the nitrous, and the wheel's would unstick. The car was a POS, but the mechanic thought that the TPS sensor was shot. You said you replaced it, but maybe the replacement is faulty?<BR>I'd also check the EGR system. That tends to cause problems on the low-end.<BR>Sean||||reg||5
Z||000004||hugh||04-28-2001||06:23 PM||||It almost sounds like the gearing is too high,or at least higher than your wife's.||||reg||1
Z||000005||WKDFIRO||04-28-2001||09:28 PM||||Im not familiar at all with 84-86 dukes, but if it were a V6 I would say to check the internals of the distributor. Do the 4 cyls have P/U coils and modules? The distributors that you had were either one of them rebuilt with new parts? <P>Im just shooting in the dark as for as your engine...Good luck to you!||||reg||5
Z||000006||Fierowrecker||04-29-2001||12:37 AM||||Hey Dave!<BR>I also had a problem with my 2.5L like yours...<BR>I replaced the coil with the Accell unit, the best wires, and AC rapidfire plugs...<BR>No problems sence...<BR>You replace the PVC valve by chance?<BR>crash...<p>[This message has been edited by Fierowrecker (edited 04-29-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000007||2farnorth||04-29-2001||08:25 AM||||The distributor cap, rotor, wires, and plugs are all new.  All the other parts came off an engine that had a bearing rap, but the throttle response was good.  I'm going to try a new coil.  I don't know much history on this engine.  I know it's not original. It's marked YAP on the block.  I'm wondering if maybe someone changed the timing gears and<BR>got it lined up one tooth off.<P>Dave||||reg||5
